        "message": {
          "role": "assistant",
          "content": "# One Year, One Day at a Time\n\nToday I’m celebrating one year sober from heroin.\n\nA year ago, I couldn’t picture writing that sentence. I wanted a different life, but wanting it and believing I could build it were two different things. Some days, getting through the next hour felt like more than I could manage.\n\nRecovery hasn’t made every day easy. I’ve had to face feelings I used to run from, ask for help when I wanted to disappear, and learn that a hard day doesn’t erase the work I’ve done. Progress has looked less like a dramatic transformation and more like a series of small choices: telling the truth, reaching out, and trying again tomorrow.\n\nI’m proud of this year. I’m also grateful for the people who stayed close and for the chance to become someone I can count on. I’m still learning, and I don’t need to pretend otherwise to celebrate how far I’ve come.\n\nIf you’re struggling, please know you don’t have to have the whole future figured out today. Ask for help. Let someone sit with you through the hard part. A life can change one decision, one conversation, one day at a time.\n\nToday, I’m here. That’s worth celebrating.",
